 \begin{abstract}
-LLM-as-a-judge evaluation is widely used for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), but reusing the same model family as both generator and judge makes self-leniency difficult to identify. We introduce \method, a controlled meta-evaluation protocol for source-grounded RAG. Starting from GaRAGe questions and grounding passages, we induce one hidden answer-causal contradiction per record, generate answers from perturbed passages with GPT, Grok, and Gemini models, and then use the same models as blind judges to evaluate each answer against the original passages. The experiment contains 300 core records, 897 labeled generator outputs, and 2,683 judge verdicts in a crossed $3\times3$ matrix; the primary analysis uses 275 fully validated records. Instead of comparing diagonal and off-diagonal cells across different answers, we estimate same-model effects by pairing judges on the exact same candidate answer. This changes the interpretation: diagonal and off-diagonal F1 are similar, and the paired same-model recall effect is near zero ($-0.5$ pp; 95\% cluster-bootstrap CI $[-2.7,+1.7]$). The only robust paired gap is lower matching-judge flagging for answers that avoided the induced claim ($-4.3$ pp). A targeted human evaluation finds that reviewed apparent false positives are alternate source-error detections, mistakes in labeling whether the induced claim was adopted, or unclear cases; none were adjudicated as genuine false alarms. The lesson is methodological: RAG judge studies should report full matrices, answer-paired effects, behavior strata, and label-task alignment.
+LLM-as-a-judge evaluation is widely used for retrieval-augmented generation (RAG), but reusing a model as generator and judge complicates identifying self-leniency. We introduce \method, a controlled meta-evaluation protocol for source-grounded RAG. Starting from GaRAGe, we induce one hidden answer-causal contradiction per record, generate answers from perturbed passages with GPT, Grok, and Gemini, and use the same models as blind judges against the original passages. The experiment contains 300 core records, 897 labeled outputs, and 2,683 verdicts in a crossed $3\times3$ matrix; the primary analysis uses 275 validated records. We estimate same-model effects by pairing judges on the exact same candidate answer. Diagonal and off-diagonal F1 are similar, and aggregate paired recall is near zero for the three tested endpoints ($-0.5$ pp; 95\% cluster-bootstrap CI $[-2.7,+1.7]$). Matching judges flag answers that avoided the induced claim less often ($-4.3$ pp). A targeted human evaluation classifies reviewed apparent false positives as alternate source-error detections, adoption-label mistakes, or unclear cases; none were adjudicated as genuine false alarms. We recommend full matrices, answer-paired effects, behavior strata, and label-task alignment.
 \end{abstract}
 
